{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L278-L290","doc":"**The transferred super-exponential bound.** For every `q > 0` satisfying the\npresentation hypothesis, the period `S` is bounded below by `(k)!` whenever `2k ≤ n` —\nfactorial growth, off `q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L265-L276","doc":"**The physical period dominates the classical one.** Same hypotheses, divided by `n`:\n`a_D^(n)(q) = S/n ≥ I(n)/n = classicalPeriod n` for every `q > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_le_of_barSymmetric_presentation","kind":"theorem","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L234-L263","doc":"**The `q`-transfer, conditional on the presentation.** Suppose the na-Stokes period\n`S(n)(q) = Σ_{λ⊢n} dim_q λ` is presented as a bar-symmetric Laurent polynomial with\nnon-negative coefficients, `S = c₀ + Σ_k c_k (q^k + q^{−k})`, whose value at `q = 1`\nis the involution number `I n` (that value is `c₀ + 2 Σ_k c_k`). Then for **every**\n`q > 0`, `I n ≤ S`.\n\nCombined with `factorial_le_involution_two_mul` this transports the whole\nsuper-exponential lower bound off `q = 1` and onto the physical pin: the period at any\n`q > 0` is at least the classical one, which is at least `(n/2)!`.\n\nThe presentation hypothesis is **verified exactly at `n = 2..9`** by\n`computations/sw_period_bar_positivity_probe.py` (balanced `[k]_q`, q-hook dimension,\nsympy exact — `S(n)` is a Laurent polynomial, bar-symmetric, with non-negative\ncoefficients, and `S(n)(1) = I(n)` at every `n`). It is **not proved for general `n`**;\nthe general statement is the q-hook-length formula\n`[n]_q!/∏[h]_q = Σ_{T ∈ SYT(λ)} q^{maj T}`, which needs the same missing\n`StandardYoungTableau` layer as beans `qou-m3xr` / `qou-jlam`.\n\nNote the transfer carries **no regime condition on `q`**: non-negativity of the\ncoefficients is what does the work. In particular it does not require `q < φ`, and the\nprobe confirms the bound at `q = φ`, `q = 2` and `q = 5`. (`q − q⁻¹ = 1` exactly at\n`q = φ` — a real feature of the balanced `q`-integer, but not a gate on this claim.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.barSymmetric_ge_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.barSymmetric_ge_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L211-L232","doc":"**A bar-symmetric Laurent polynomial with non-negative coefficients is minimised at\n`q = 1`.** Writing `P(q) = c₀ + Σ_k c_k (q^k + q^{−k})` with every `c_k ≥ 0`, each\npaired term obeys `q^k + q^{−k} ≥ 2` (`two_le_add_inv` at `x = q^k`), so\n`P(q) ≥ c₀ + 2 Σ_k c_k = P(1)`.\n\nThis is the general-`q` half of the transfer `a_D^(n)(q) ≥ a_D^(n)(1)`: the na-Stokes\nperiod `S(n) = Σ_{λ⊢n} dim_q λ` is bar-invariant, so *if* its coefficients are\nnon-negative the classical bound above is a bound for every `q > 0`. Verified\nnumerically on the corpus's own closed forms — `lw(2) = (q+1)²/(3q)` and\n`lw(3) = 5/2 + (3/4)(q−q⁻¹)²` both attain their minimum `I(c+1)/(c+1)` exactly at\n`q = 1`. The missing piece is the presentation of `dim_q λ` in this form, which needs\nthe SYT infrastructure Mathlib does not have. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.two_le_add_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.two_le_add_inv","kind":"theorem","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L204-L209","doc":"For `x > 0`, `2 ≤ x + x⁻¹` — the AM–GM step, `x + x⁻¹ − 2 = (x−1)²/x ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_ratio_unbounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_ratio_unbounded","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L179-L200","doc":"**The period's growth leaves every geometric class.** For every `C`, past `n = C`\nthe period's two-step ratio exceeds `C/2`; since a geometric sequence has constant\nratio, no geometric sequence dominates `a_D^(n)|_{q=1}`. (The `n/(n+2)` factor from the\n`1/n` normalisation is bounded below by `1/2` for `n ≥ 2`, hence the `C/2`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod_pos","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L171-L177","doc":"The classical period is **strictly positive** for `n ≥ 1` — the derived form of the\n`0 ≤ a_D^(n)` hypothesis that `QOU.Mass.JetFiltrationExtensive` carries abstractly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.classicalPeriod","kind":"def","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L167-L169","doc":"The classical (`q = 1`) binding period of `prop:sw-classical-jet-exactness`:\n`a_D^(n)|_{q=1} = lw(n−1) = I(n)/n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_involution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_involution","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L160-L163","doc":"`n ! ≤ I m` for every `m ≥ 2n`: the even bound, transported by monotonicity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_mono","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L157-L158","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_le_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_le_succ","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L148-L155","doc":"`I` is monotone: `I(n+2) = I(n+1) + (n+1)·I(n) ≥ I(n+1)`, since the added term is a\nnatural number. Transports the even-index bound to every index. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involutionNumber_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involutionNumber_pos","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L138-L146","doc":"`I n > 0` for every `n`: the recurrence only ever adds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_involution_two_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.factorial_le_involution_two_mul","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L119-L136","doc":"**Quantitative form: `n ! ≤ I (2n)`.** Iterating the step along the even\nsubsequence multiplies by `2n+1 ≥ n+1` at each stage, so the even-indexed involution\nnumbers grow at least factorially. This is a genuine super-exponential *lower* bound —\nthe direction `involution_le_factorial` does not provide. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_ratio_unbounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_ratio_unbounded","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L108-L117","doc":"**`I` leaves every geometric class.** For any constant `C`, past `n = C` the\ntwo-step ratio exceeds `C`. A geometric sequence has *constant* two-step ratio, so no\ngeometric sequence dominates `I` — this is the corpus's reading of \"super-exponential\"\nin `prop:tower-geometric-lower-bound`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.mul_involution_le_involution_add_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.mul_involution_le_involution_add_two","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L100-L106","doc":"**The one load-bearing step.** Dropping the `I(n+1)` summand from the recurrence\nleaves `(n+1)·I n ≤ I (n+2)`: the two-step growth factor is at least `n+1`, which is\n*not* bounded by any constant. Everything below is this inequality iterated. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_succ_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SWPeriodSuperExponential.involution_succ_succ","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SWPeriodSuperExponential.lean#L95-L98","doc":"The defining recurrence, as an equation (definitional, restated for `omega`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}